WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.167
Query optimization.

2
00:00:02.467 --> 00:00:06.688
A slow query is usually the database reading far more than it needs to.

3
00:00:06.688 --> 00:00:08.233
Let's see how to catch it.

4
00:00:09.433 --> 00:00:11.500
The database is honest if you ask it.

5
00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.034
EXPLAIN shows the plan it chose.

6
00:00:14.034 --> 00:00:17.733
EXPLAIN ANALYZE runs the query and adds real timings.

7
00:00:18.033 --> 00:00:23.000
The pattern to hunt for is a sequential scan on a big table, where an index scan should be.

8
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.767
This is the plan flip you are engineering for.

9
00:00:27.067 --> 00:00:30.267
First, a sequential scan reads every row of the table.

10
00:00:30.567 --> 00:00:34.967
Then you create an index, a sorted B-tree over the columns you filter on.

11
00:00:35.267 --> 00:00:38.833
Now the engine descends the tree straight to the rows that match.

12
00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:41.633
Here is the difference in time.

13
00:00:41.933 --> 00:00:45.641
The sequential scan took nine hundred forty two milliseconds.

14
00:00:45.641 --> 00:00:49.167
With the index, the same query took under one millisecond.

15
00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:53.633
One catch: the query must be written so the index can help.

16
00:00:53.933 --> 00:00:57.367
A range on the raw timestamp column can use the index.

17
00:00:57.667 --> 00:01:00.967
But wrapping the column in a function hides it from the index.

18
00:01:01.267 --> 00:01:03.338
Wrapped columns cause a full scan.

19
00:01:03.338 --> 00:01:04.800
Rewrite them as a range.

20
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.900
Here is the whole workflow in code.

21
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:11.633
We run EXPLAIN ANALYZE and see a slow sequential scan.

22
00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:15.333
Then we build a composite index on the two filter columns.

23
00:01:15.633 --> 00:01:20.667
Rerun it, and the plan becomes an index scan that finishes in under a millisecond.

24
00:01:21.867 --> 00:01:23.200
Here is a real case.

25
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:27.819
A RAG system filters chunks by mission and date before vector search.

26
00:01:27.819 --> 00:01:32.200
At four million chunks, that filter alone takes one point two seconds.

27
00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:37.300
One EXPLAIN ANALYZE reveals the full scan, and one composite index fixes it.

28
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:41.433
Latency falls under ten milliseconds, from fifteen minutes of work.

29
00:01:42.633 --> 00:01:43.467
Let's recap.

30
00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:45.967
EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows the real plan.

31
00:01:46.267 --> 00:01:48.833
Replace sequential scans with index scans.

32
00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:52.700
Keep predicates sargable, with no functions around columns.

33
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.446
And index what you filter and join on.

34
00:01:55.446 --> 00:01:56.733
Next up: PostgreSQL.
